How sunspots control global weather
The relationship between sunspots and global weather is a fascinating interplay that has intrigued scientists for decades. Sunspots, which are temporary phenomena on the Sun's surface appearing as dark spots, follow an 11-year cycle, influencing numerous aspects of our climate. This data-driven story explores how the number of sunspots corresponds to variations in cosmic rays, solar irradiation, and global temperatures. By analyzing historical data on sunspots alongside the amount of cosmic rays and solar energy reaching Earth, we uncover the potential mechanisms by which these celestial activities can impact weather patterns on Earth. The data supports the narrative that increased sunspot activity leads to greater solar energy, reduced cosmic rays, and consequently, significant effects on global temperatures and weather phenomena.
