Low-Income and Minority Students at U.S. Colleges
The challenges faced by low-income and minority students in navigating higher education in the U.S. are significant and multifaceted. This data-driven story examines the relationship between college selectivity, average SAT scores, and the representation of Pell grant recipients—predominantly affecting students from lower-income households—across various institutions. The analysis showcases public colleges, highlighting how higher SAT scores correlate with a lower percentage of students receiving Pell grants, implying financial barriers that hinder access. In contrast, minority enrollment rises with Pell rates, indicating a complex interplay between race, class, and educational opportunity. Additionally, the exploration of private institutions reveals a greater disparity in financial aid distribution, with selective colleges tending to enroll fewer Pell recipients and minority students, underlining the systemic challenges that persist in the pursuit of equitable education.
