Flight Delays
This data-driven story delves into the trends and patterns of flight delays across the United States, drawing from comprehensive datasets on total flights, delay percentages, and delay causes from January 2013 to August 2017. It reveals key insights such as peak flight numbers occurring in July, with February holding the lowest counts, along with significant fluctuations in delay percentages, notably peaking during the summer and winter months. The analysis identifies late arrival delays as the most frequent cause, with carrier and NAS delays following. Moreover, it highlights differences in delay patterns based on the airport size and geographical location, demonstrating how these factors influence the overall flying experience.
